{=============================================================================
  External forced convection over a flat plate
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0101 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Four Nusselt-number correlations for an isothermal flat plate in a crossflow
  at constant surface temperature: two laminar forms (Baehr-Stephan, piecewise in
  the Prandtl number, and Churchill-Ozoe, valid over the whole Prandtl range)
  and two fully turbulent forms (Schlichting, with the surface-friction term of
  that formulation, and Kreith).

  All of them return the average Nusselt number of the plate, on its length L;
  the heat-transfer coefficient follows from h = Nu*k/L and is left to the
  caller. All inputs are dimensionless: Re (Re_L, plate length and bulk fluid
  properties) and Pr (bulk temperature). No length appears in the arguments: the
  plate length enters through Re, as in ht.

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function, once with the
  inputs of the ht doctest or regression test of the corresponding correlation
  ("A" cases) and once with a second, uniform input set ("B" cases). The ht
  values of both sets are tabulated in the README (verification table).

  Three correlations of the module are only dispatchers in ht
  (Nu_external_horizontal_plate, Nu_external_horizontal_plate_methods: they
  select a method by name, and conv_horizontal_plate_methods only holds the
  four functions of this file) and are not translated; an EES caller selects the
  function directly. The laminar-turbulent transition Reynolds number of ht
  (LAMINAR_TRANSITION_HORIZONTAL_PLATE = 5E5) is not a correlation either: it is
  a constant of the calling model.

  Inputs : Re [-], Pr [-]
  Outputs: Nu [-] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
  Units  : SI - dimensionless numbers
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_external.py, version 1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6,
           MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations by the authors
           cited in each function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re, Pr)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_LAMINAR_BAEHR - Nusselt number, laminar flow across an
 isothermal flat plate. No other wall correction is necessary for this
 formulation; four equations are used for different Prandtl number ranges.

  Pr < 0.005      : Nu_L = 1.128*Re^0.5*Pr^0.5
  Pr < 0.05       : Nu_L = 1.0*Re^0.5*Pr^0.5
  Pr < 10         : Nu_L = 0.664*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3)
  otherwise       : Nu_L = 0.678*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3)

 Re [-] is on the plate length with the bulk fluid properties and Pr [-] is at
 the bulk temperature. The 0.664 equation is also recommended in the Incropera
 and VDI Heat Atlas references of the original.
 Validity: the four Prandtl branches above (as in ht); the free convection over
  the plate, which can increase the convection substantially, is not taken into
  account. The transition to turbulent flow is not treated here either (ht uses
  Re_L = 5E5, LAMINAR_TRANSITION_HORIZONTAL_PLATE).
 Original: Baehr, H. D. and K. Stephan, Heat and Mass Transfer, Springer, 2013;
   also shown in Bergman, T. L., A. S. Lavine, F. P. Incropera and D. P. DeWitt,
   Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E, Wiley, 2011, and in the VDI Heat Atlas,
   2nd ed. 2010 edition, Berlin/New York, Springer (equation G4).
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_external.py,
   Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr, MIT License, C. Bell -
   https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  "Non-smooth regime switches are written with nested IF blocks (no ELSEIF)"
  "The branch limits are those of the ht code; its docstring quotes 0.6 < Pr
   for the 0.664 equation (as in the original)."
  IF Pr < 0.005 THEN
    Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr = 1.128*(Re*Pr)^0.5
  ELSE
    IF Pr < 0.05 THEN
      Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr = (Re*Pr)^0.5
    ELSE
      IF Pr < 10 THEN
        Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr = 0.664*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3)
      ELSE
        Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr = 0.678*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3)
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe(Re, Pr)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_LAMINAR_CHURCHILL_OZOE - Nusselt number, laminar flow
 across an isothermal flat plate. No other wall correction is necessary for this
 formulation; a single equation covers all Prandtl number ranges.

  Nu_L = 0.6774*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3) / [1 + (0.0468/Pr)^(2/3)]^0.25

 Re [-] is on the plate length with the bulk fluid properties and Pr [-] is at
 the bulk temperature.
 Validity: laminar flow; no limit is quoted (a single equation covers all
 Prandtl numbers); the free convection over the plate, which can increase the
 convection substantially, is not taken into account.
 Original: Churchill, S. W. and H. Ozoe, "Correlations for Laminar Forced
   Convection in Flow Over an Isothermal Flat Plate and in Developing and Fully
   Developed Flow in an Isothermal Tube", Journal of Heat Transfer 95(3): 416,
   1 August 1973, doi:10.1115/1.3450078; also shown in Bergman, T. L., A. S.
   Lavine, F. P. Incropera and D. P. DeWitt, Introduction to Heat Transfer, 6E,
   Wiley, 2011.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_external.py,
   Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe, MIT License, C. Bell -
   https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe = 0.6774*Re^0.5*Pr^(1/3)/(1 + (0.0468/Pr)^(2/3))^0.25
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting(Re, Pr)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_TURBULENT_SCHLICHTING - Nusselt number, turbulent flow
 across an isothermal flat plate. The formulation of Schlichting is used, which
 adds a surface friction term to a formulation from Petukhov and Popov.

  Nu_L = 0.037*Re^0.8*Pr / {1 + 2.443*Re^(-0.1)*(Pr^(2/3) - 1)}

 Re [-] is on the plate length with the bulk fluid properties and Pr [-] is at
 the bulk temperature.
 Validity: turbulent flow only; no limit is quoted; the free convection over the
 plate, which can increase the convection substantially, is not taken into
 account.
 Original: Schlichting, H. and K. Gersten, Grenzschicht-Theorie, 9th ed.,
   Springer-Verlag, Berlin Heidelberg, 1997; also quoted in the VDI Heat Atlas,
   2nd ed. 2010 edition, Berlin/New York, Springer.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_external.py,
   Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting, MIT License, C. Bell -
   https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting = 0.037*Re^0.8*Pr/(1 + 2.443*Re^(-0.1)*(Pr^(2/3) - 1))
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith(Re, Pr)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_TURBULENT_KREITH - Nusselt number, turbulent flow across
 an isothermal flat plate.

  Nu_L = 0.036*Re^0.8*Pr^(1/3)

 Re [-] is on the plate length with the bulk fluid properties and Pr [-] is at
 the bulk temperature.
 Validity: turbulent flow only (as in the original); no limit is quoted; the
 free convection over the plate, which can increase the convection substantially,
 is not taken into account.
 Original: Kreith, F., R. Manglik and M. Bohn, Principles of Heat Transfer,
   Cengage, 2010.
 Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_external.py,
   Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith, MIT License, C. Bell -
   https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith = 0.036*Re^0.8*Pr^(1/3)
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:external_forced_conv_plates (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input sets of the ht doctests and of the ht regression tests of each"
"  correlation (published values); all four Prandtl branches of Baehr are exercised."
"Case B: one second, uniform input set (Re = 5E6, Pr = 0.7 - air in crossflow over a plate)."
"Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README (verification table)."

"Case A - input sets"
Re_A = 1E5
Pr_A = 0.7
Re_Kreith_A = 1.03E6
Pr_Kreith_A = 0.71

"Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr - the four Prandtl branches at Re = 1E5 (Pr = 0.01 is the 0.005 to 0.05 branch, computed with the same ht function; the other four are the published ht values)"
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A_Pr_1e_4 = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, 1E-4)
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A_Pr_0p01 = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, 0.01)
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A_Pr_0p1 = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, 0.1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A_Pr1 = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A_Pr100 = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, 100)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr - the doctest, Pr = 0.7"
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe - the doctest"
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting - the doctest"
Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting(Re_A, Pr_A)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith - the doctest"
Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith(Re_Kreith_A, Pr_Kreith_A)

"Case B - second, uniform input set"
Re_B = 5E6
Pr_B = 0.7

"Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr - far above the laminar range of the correlation"
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Baehr(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe - far above the laminar range of the correlation"
Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_laminar_Churchill_Ozoe(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting - turbulent range of the correlation"
Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Schlichting(Re_B, Pr_B)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith - turbulent range of the correlation"
Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_turbulent_Kreith(Re_B, Pr_B)
